<title>JAPAN: Line-up of candidates in Japan general elections.</title>
<headline>Line-up of candidates in Japan general elections.</headline>
<dateline>TOKYO 1996-10-10</dateline>
<text>
<p>A record 1,503 candidates are running for the 500 Lower House seats in the October 20 general elections, the first employing a complex system of 300 single-seat constituencies and 200 seats through proportional representation in 11 regional blocs.</p>
<p>Under the new system, the number of Lower House seats has been reduced to 500 from 511. Candidates are allowed to run both in a constituency and be placed on the proportional representation list, indicated by &quot;Both&quot; below, but may only occupy one seat if they win in both.</p>
<p>    PARTY.............Total..Single..Propor..Both..Incumbent</p>
<p>    LDP...............355....288.....328.....261....211</p>
<p>    Shinshinto........361....235.....133.......7....160</p>
<p>    Democrats.........161....143.....159.....141.....52</p>
<p>    Socialists.........48.....43......48......43.....30</p>
<p>    Communists........321....299......53......31.....15</p>
<p>    Sakigake...........15.....13......11.......9......9</p>
<p>    Minor parties.....157....155......77......75......6</p>
<p>    Independents.......85.....85......10</p>
<p>    Vacant ............18</p>
<p>---------------------------------------------------</p>
<p>    Total...........1,503..1,261.....809.....567....511</p>
</text>
